    "created_at": "2021-02-26T16:44:04Z",
    "updated_at": "2021-02-26T16:44:04Z",
    "body": "Sure. This proposal came out of the realization that many people asked for it in the context of declarative Shadow DOM. E.g. [this comment](https://github.com/whatwg/dom/issues/831#issuecomment-585254228) and many others on the same issue. It is a common request to \"not have to copy/paste the same thing around a page\". But as a feature, it really has nothing to do with DSD or Web Components.\r\n\r\nThe use case is either SSR'd content with repeated components, or hand-authored repeated content. In both cases, you want to declare a chunk of HTML, and then be able to easily (and with few bytes and no Javascript) declaratively stamp it out on the page. I agree with you on two fronts:\r\n 1. Being able to change the data for each instance would definitely be nice, and might be necessary, for this to be useful. A la declarative parts. Until that is fleshed out further, I'm not sure how this proposal would interoperate with that one. Off the cuff, perhaps part values could be specified with additional attributes on the `<div clone=foo>` tag?\r\n 2. Declarative web components also tries to solve a similar problem, and could likely be used for the above purpose, but is quite a bit more heavyweight. (Not to mention not at all defined.) This feature is intended to be a low-level general-purpose API, not tied to custom elements. Perhaps this feature could be used as a primitive to build the eventual declarative Custom Elements proposal?",
    "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
    "pin": null,
