    "created_at": "2021-03-15T15:22:17Z",
    "updated_at": "2021-03-15T15:24:29Z",
    "body": "Maybe I missed the point but somehow this reminds me of XSL/T - much reinventing the wheel?\r\n\r\nIn the case of XML/XSL the server would send XML data and an XSL Template and the browser would create the content tags, be it shadow-dom-ed or not. In HTML5 as application/xml this would work perfectly fine.\r\n\r\nSo browser support for extended XSL/Xpath is what is missing?\r\n\r\n***\r\n\r\nIf XSLT and/or XPATH are deemed dead because it is XML and not JSON and ugly/hard to learn, I'd still encourage you to look up what it did/does and reimplement -- in a better, elegant, more approachable, more readable, still declarative way -- what it did great, such as the use case you have here.",
