    "created_at": "2021-03-18T16:52:32Z",
    "updated_at": "2021-03-18T16:52:32Z",
    "body": "Very interesting.\r\n\r\nI'd be a little wary of developing this completely separate from declarative custom elements. It seems easy to go down a path where this builds up into another component model. In my experience, most shadow roots for the same class of element are not identical, so there would very quickly be a need for expressions/interpolations. At that point, if this is intended to be used for declarative shadow DOM, what we have is essentially the guts of declarative custom elements, without the element declaration part.\r\n\r\nDeclarative custom elements would also help answer question (1): Children of the component are left in place and slotted as per the existing semantics.\r\n\r\nRe shared styles: I don't think this would be great for shared styles among declarative shadow roots. What we want there is to actually share a stylesheet like adopted stylesheets allows us to do. Copying/cloning a style tag doesn't give the same semantics. And if we found a way to reference a common stylesheet, we should be able to use that references directly on the `<template shadowroot>` element to adopt the stylesheet, as I sketched out here: https://github.com/whatwg/dom/issues/831#issuecomment-585505923\r\n\r\n```html\r\n<html>\r\n\r\n  <style type=\"adopted-css\" id=\"style-one\">\r\n    /* ... */\r\n  </style>\r\n\r\n  <style type=\"adopted-css\" id=\"style-two\">\r\n    /* ... */\r\n  </style>\r\n\r\n  <div>\r\n    <template shadowroot=\"open\" adopted-styles=\"style-one style-two\">\r\n      <!-- ... -->\r\n    </template>\r\n  </div>\r\n</html>\r\n```\r\n\r\n",
    "author_association": "NONE",
